"""Plain-English conclusions shown in the report (written 16 Oct 2026 after the full run)."""

OVERVIEW = "<h3>The short version</h3><ul>\n<li><b>Week-ahead fades (power and gas) don't pay.</b> Wind forecasts are reliable out to about 5 days, so a market that believes them is mostly right; only days 6-7 need pulling back, and that is too small after costs.</li>\n<li><b>The forecasts are still valuable for power:</b> our weather-based fair price predicts next week's power price far better than recent prices (about £14 vs £17.5/MWh miss).</li>\n<li><b>Gas barely responds to the week-ahead weather</b> compared with everything else that moves it.</li>\n<li><b>The month/quarter-ahead fade is promising.</b> Betting that recent unusual weather won't carry into next month beat a spot-anchored price on all four products (power and gas, month and quarter) in the test period. How much real forward prices anchor on spot is the open question.</li></ul>"

FINDINGS = {'power_week_ahead': "The weather <i>does</i> predict next week's power price well. Our fair price misses by about £14/MWh, against £17.5 for 'same as last week'. But the <b>fade itself</b> (betting against a market that believes the forecast) found almost nothing to fade. Wind forecasts turn out to be trustworthy out to 4-5 days; only days 6 and 7 need pulling back (trust factor 0.87 and 0.69). Averaged over a whole Mon-Sun week, that pull-back rarely moves the price by more than the £2/MWh needed to trade. In the test year the pure fade made <b>no trades</b> on the week product, and <b>lost</b> on single days 6 days out (t = -2.1). The big profits in the table come from markets that partly <i>ignore</i> the forecast, which a real market doesn't do much. <b>Verdict: no tradeable fade.</b> The useful thing built here is the fair-value model: compared against a real broker quote, it would show whether the market ever drifts from the forecast.", 'nbp_week_ahead': "Weather barely helps predict next week's gas price. The weather model's error is 10.5p/therm against 10.6 for 'same as last week' in the test year. The model learned sensible effects (colder weeks, especially on the Continent, raise prices), but they are small next to everything else that moves gas: LNG cargoes, Norway, storage, geopolitics. The pure fade never found a gap worth trading. <b>Verdict: no signal.</b> NBP is more liquid than UK power, but liquidity doesn't help if the edge isn't there.", 'weather_month_ahead': "The longer-term fade works on a different principle. Weather can't be forecast a month out, but unusual weather also doesn't persist, so next month should look like a normal month at today's weather-neutral price, not like the last two weeks. That held up. Our fair price was usually more accurate than recent spot: for gas quarters it missed by 36% against 43-49% in tuning and 18% against 19-20% in the test; for power months 18% against 19% vs the 14-day average in the test. Against a market fully anchored on recent spot, the fade made money in the test period on <b>all four products</b> (power month: 37 trades, 62% winners, t = 2.3). Against a market only half-anchored, results were mixed and sometimes lost. <b>Verdict: promising, unproven.</b> The direction is consistent, but the profit depends on how much real month-ahead prices lean on recent spot, which only real quotes can show. Few data points: 89 months, 28 quarters."}
